## 1. GRANT OF LICENSE

Subject to all the terms and conditions of this EULA, OPTIMAJET grants Developer End User a non-exclusive, worldwide license, not transferable except as Section 5 and the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph of Section 1.1 permit, to install and use the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S). Section 1.1 sets out what a commercial license covers, Section 1.3 sets out the rights to publicly available Source Code and to other Source Code provided under a license that includes source code access, and Section 17 sets out the usage types. A Form Engine license included in, or purchased with, an edition of Workflow Engine NEO covers the Licensed Product of that Workflow Engine NEO license within the Licensed Use of Form Engine Enterprise; its License Statement states whether it is perpetual or an annual subscription and whether it includes access to Source Code other than Public Source Code. Every paid license, including one included in or purchased with Workflow Engine NEO, permits use of Public Source Code and Modified Software under Section 1.3 within that license’s scope. A Form Engine license included in an edition of Workflow Engine NEO has the term of that Workflow Engine NEO license, and a termination of that license ends the Form Engine license, except a termination by OPTIMAJET for a breach that does not concern Form Engine.

## 1.1 LICENSED PRODUCT AND LICENSED USE

DEFINITIONS. The following definitions apply throughout this EULA:

- “License Statement” means the document that OPTIMAJET issues for a purchase, such as a Statement of License or, for a purchase in an online store, the message with the license key or the invoice. It names, as they apply to the purchase, Developer End User, the license purchased and the packages that it covers, its Licensed Use, the Licensed Product where the license requires it or where an integrator buys the license for a client, the End Customer whose copy the license covers under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph, the purchaser where it is not Developer End User, the Domains, the update period, any later warranty start date, the Source Code Window where the license includes source code access, the term of an annual subscription, the options purchased, any installment plan, the license contact and any legal contact and security contact, the public bodies named under the PUBLIC BODIES paragraph, and the version of this EULA. A License Statement for an upgrade names the license it replaces.
- “Licensed Product” means a software application or service of Developer End User, or of the client for which Developer End User holds a license under the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph, into which the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) is integrated, or the copy of another person’s product, such as an integrator’s, into which the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) is integrated and that Developer End User licenses under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph; for a Public Web Application / SaaS and for OEM use, it must add significant functionality of its own. Its versions, modules, and editions for different platforms, such as web, desktop, iOS, and Android, that are developed and delivered as one product are one Licensed Product. Products or modules that Developer End User or its Affiliates offer to End Customers as separate products, each of which an End Customer can buy, subscribe to, or use without the others, are separate Licensed Products, even where they share code, a brand, an installer, or a service.
- “Licensed Use” means the usage types under Section 17 that the purchased license permits, as stated in Section 18, in OPTIMAJET’s price list on the date of purchase, and in the License Statement.
- “price list” means the licenses, prices, and terms that OPTIMAJET publishes on formengine.io (or, for a Form Engine license included in or purchased with an edition of Workflow Engine NEO, on workflowengine.io) on the date of purchase (for a renewal, on the date of that renewal) or, for a license whose prices OPTIMAJET does not publish there, the list of prices or the quote that OPTIMAJET provides for that purchase, read together with the terms published there.
- “Production Use” means use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) in a live system that processes the real data of Developer End User, of its Affiliates, or of its End Customers. Any other use, including development, testing, staging, continuous integration, demonstration, and training, and use in a standby system that takes over only when the live system fails, is “Non-Production Use.” Testing, including acceptance and load testing, debugging, migration rehearsals, and training on a copy of production data are Non-Production Use, and a pilot with real users is Production Use.
- “Domain” means a domain name under which a Licensed Product serves Form Engine Designer to its users in Production Use. A wildcard Domain covers the subdomains of that domain.
- “Affiliate” means an entity that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with Developer End User, where control means ownership of more than fifty percent (50%) of the voting interests, other than common control by an investment fund or its manager, or by a state, a government, or a public body, for as long as that control lasts. For six (6) months after control ends, a former Affiliate may continue the Production Use that it made under the license of Developer End User, without new deployments.
- “public body” means a state, a regional or local government, an international organization, and a ministry, department, agency, or other body that is part of any of them or is governed by public law and exercises public functions, including a public university or hospital; a company that a public body owns or controls and that sells its products or services on the market on the same terms as other companies is a public body only for the PUBLIC PROCUREMENT LAW paragraph of Section 16, where the law that governs its procurement applies to it.
- “Authorized Contractor” means an individual or a company, including a partner of OPTIMAJET, that works, directly or as a subcontractor at any tier, for Developer End User or its Affiliate on a Licensed Product under a written agreement.
- “End Customer” means a third party that uses a Licensed Product, including as a hosted service, or receives it from Developer End User. A hosting provider or another contractor that runs a copy only for Developer End User or its Affiliates, or whose individuals use a Licensed Product only as Own Users, is not an End Customer.
- “Source Code” means the human-readable source code of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) that OPTIMAJET makes available under this EULA, with its build scripts and comments. “Public Source Code” means the Source Code that OPTIMAJET expressly publishes for public access under this EULA, including the Source Code of Form Engine Designer. It does not include code made public without OPTIMAJET’s authorization, private license keys, or code governed by a separate license under the second paragraph of this EULA or Section 7.
- “Source Code Window” means the update period of a license, including any renewal, during which Developer End User is entitled to the versions of the Source Code covered by that license, as stated in the price list or the License Statement. For Source Code other than Public Source Code, access also requires a license that includes that access. The Source Code Window limits the versions covered for use under a paid license; it does not limit access to Public Source Code or the separate free rights in the PUBLIC SOURCE CODE paragraph of Section 1.3.
- “Modifications” means the changes and additions that Developer End User makes to the Source Code. “Modified Software” means the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) together with Modifications, in source code or built form. A translation, port, or other rewrite of the Source Code, or of a substantial part of it, into another form or programming language, made by any means, including an AI tool, is Modified Software that contains the Source Code, and not Modifications alone.
- “net price” of a license, a renewal, or another purchase means the price paid to OPTIMAJET for it, less the taxes that OPTIMAJET collected with that payment or that were withheld from it, and the payment processing, bank, and currency conversion fees that OPTIMAJET bears for that payment.
- “confidential information” of OPTIMAJET means the Source Code other than Public Source Code and the non-public information that OPTIMAJET marks as confidential when it gives it to Developer End User; the confidential information of Developer End User is its code, data, and product descriptions, other than a contribution under the CONTRIBUTIONS paragraph of Section 1.3, and the information that it gives OPTIMAJET for support, for a confirmation under this EULA, for an installation, or for the registration of a project. Information of Developer End User that is or becomes public other than through a breach by OPTIMAJET is not its confidential information.
- “business day” means a day other than a Saturday, a Sunday, or a public holiday in Hong Kong.
- “Competing Product” has the meaning given in Section 1.2, “Protected Code” in Section 1.4, “Own Users” in Section 17.2, “Redistributables” in Section 6, the “liability limit” in Section 13, and “IP Claim” in Section 14.

LICENSED USE. Each commercial license covers one Licensed Product, for the Licensed Use of the license purchased. Under Sections 17.3 and 17.4, the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) is a component of the Licensed Product, which Developer End User or its Affiliates provide to End Customers as their own product, subject to Section 1.2; that component may be invisible to End Customers, other than the notices that this EULA requires. End Customers use the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) only as part of the Licensed Product, under the terms that point (b) of the DISTRIBUTION AS PART OF A LICENSED PRODUCT paragraph of Section 6 requires; that use is covered by the license of Developer End User, within its Licensed Use and while it is in force, and afterward as Section 15 provides, and End Customers need no license from OPTIMAJET for it and do not accept this EULA. Developer End User may let End Customers design, configure, fill in, and manage their own forms through the user interface, the APIs, and the integrations of the Licensed Product, including the embedded Form Engine Designer, within point (ii) of the WHAT IS NOT A COMPETING PRODUCT paragraph of Section 1.2. A Public Web Application / SaaS, or another separate product, of Developer End User or of its Affiliate that uses a Licensed Product through its interfaces needs its own license under Section 17.3, as if the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) were integrated into it. An internal application of Developer End User or of its Affiliate that does not contain the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) and uses a Licensed Product only through its user interface or its APIs, for example to create or submit forms, is covered by the license of that Licensed Product. Where a Public Web Application / SaaS has its own license under Section 17.3, the use of a Licensed Product through it is covered by that license and does not change the Licensed Use that the Licensed Product itself needs. Developer End User may describe an application to OPTIMAJET in writing and ask which usage type under Section 17 it needs or whether it is part of a Licensed Product; OPTIMAJET will answer in writing within fifteen (15) business days, and a written answer that states that it is given under this paragraph binds OPTIMAJET for that application as described. If OPTIMAJET does not answer in time, its silence is not a confirmation. If a License Statement does not name the Licensed Product, the Licensed Product is the first product of Developer End User that uses the license in Production Use, and Developer End User will name it to OPTIMAJET in writing on request.

NON-PRODUCTION USE. Non-Production Use of a Licensed Product is included in its license at no additional fee. Domain names and hosts used only for Non-Production Use, including localhost, are not Domains and do not count toward the domain limits of the license; on request, OPTIMAJET will include them in the license key in addition to the Domains. A domain used only by a standby system that takes over only when the live system fails does not count toward the domain limits of the license.

PEOPLE. Developer End User may let its employees, the employees of its Affiliates, and its Authorized Contractors use the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) on its behalf for a Licensed Product. A license does not limit the number of developers. Developer End User is responsible for the compliance of those persons with this EULA. Except as the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph lets a contractor hold a license for a client and as the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph provides for the copies installed for End Customers, a license for a Licensed Product owned by an organization is issued to that organization, and every license for internal commercial use is issued to the organization that uses it; except as the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph provides, a license issued to an individual covers only that individual’s own product. The rights of a person working for Developer End User under Developer End User’s license end when that work ends, and that license and its license keys stay with Developer End User. Any separate rights that the person holds in Public Source Code under Section 1.3 are unaffected. Developer End User will notify OPTIMAJET when its license contact changes; OPTIMAJET will then move any private source code access to the new license contact and, on request, reissue the License Statement naming the new license contact, at no charge.

INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS. Where an integrator or another contractor, including a partner of OPTIMAJET, builds a Licensed Product for a client (the organization for which the Licensed Product is built), except as the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph provides, the license is issued to the client, the contractor may buy and pay for it on the client’s behalf, and the contractor works on the Licensed Product as the client’s Authorized Contractor. A contractor, or a person who works for it, that installs, uses, or orders the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) for a client does not accept this EULA for the client and makes no representation of authority for it; that use is covered by the client’s license, by a license that the contractor holds for the client under the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph, or by the EVALUATION paragraph. After the handover, the contractor may work on the Licensed Product only as the client’s Authorized Contractor and may not use the client’s license, license keys, or Source Code other than Public Source Code for any other client or product. Public Source Code may be used for another client or product only under the separate rights that this EULA grants for that use. Each separate product of a client needs its own license. A product that Developer End User, including an integrator, owns and installs for one or more End Customers is its own Licensed Product. Its installations in the End Customers’ environments are covered either by one license of Developer End User that includes OEM use under Section 17.4, which covers them all, or by a license of each End Customer for the copy installed for it, with that End Customer’s Licensed Use and with the product of Developer End User named as the Licensed Product in its License Statement. Developer End User may instead hold, in its own name, a license for each such copy, with the Licensed Use that the End Customer’s use of the copy would need if the End Customer were Developer End User, read with the End Customer’s own Own Users, Affiliates, and environment and, for a public body, with the PUBLIC BODIES paragraph, and with the End Customer named in its License Statement. Developer End User may assign such a license, by written notice to OPTIMAJET, to that End Customer or to another person that the End Customer names to take over that copy, if the assignee accepts this EULA in writing; the license then covers only that copy, within the same Licensed Use and usage limits, its access to Source Code other than Public Source Code passes only to an assignee that does not develop or provide a Competing Product, and OPTIMAJET will reissue its License Statement in the name of the assignee at no charge. Where the End Customers or Developer End User hold such licenses, Developer End User may develop, test, and support its product for all of them in Non-Production Use under any of those licenses, each copy runs in Production Use under its own license and license key, and Modified Software made from Source Code other than Public Source Code may be installed only for End Customers whose copies are covered by licenses that include access to that code. Modified Software made only from Public Source Code may be installed under any paid license that covers the copy, within that license’s scope and covered versions. A license of Developer End User for internal commercial use or for a Public Web Application / SaaS does not cover those installations. A product is the integrator’s own where the integrator keeps the copyright in it and licenses it to its clients; a product that the integrator delivers under a contract that assigns it to the client, or that it develops for one client’s own use and does not license to anyone else, is the client’s, whoever holds its copyright. If the integrator instead runs its own product as a service for its clients, that is a Public Web Application / SaaS under Section 17.3. A license bought through a reseller is issued to the client named in the License Statement; a reseller is not OPTIMAJET’s agent and cannot change this EULA. OPTIMAJET issues a License Statement once the price is paid or on credit terms that it chooses; once issued, a license is not terminated for a failure of a reseller or a contractor to pay OPTIMAJET, whose remedy for that failure is against the purchaser. Where a purchaser pays for a perpetual license in installments, the license runs for the period paid until it is paid in full, as its License Statement states, and Developer End User may pay the balance to OPTIMAJET directly to keep the license. A refund under this EULA is paid to the person who paid the price or, if that person has ceased to do business, to Developer End User.

PAYMENT. Developer End User will pay the price of each license, renewal, and option that it buys, as its quote, its order, or the price list states. A failure to pay, within thirty (30) days after OPTIMAJET’s written notice that it is overdue, an amount that is due and not disputed in good faith is a material breach of this EULA in relation to the license concerned, and until the amount is paid OPTIMAJET need not issue new license keys, updates other than the security updates that mandatory law requires OPTIMAJET to provide free of charge, or source code access for that license. This paragraph does not apply to a failure of a reseller or a contractor to pay, which the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph governs. An amount that is due under this EULA and not paid when due, including a refund, bears simple interest at five percent (5%) a year from its due date until it is paid.

TAXES. Unless the quote or the price list states otherwise, prices exclude taxes, duties, and withholdings, which Developer End User will pay, other than taxes on OPTIMAJET’s net income. Where the law requires Developer End User to withhold tax from a payment, it will increase the payment so that OPTIMAJET receives the price stated.

CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES. Where the client asks for it or has not yet accepted this EULA, the contractor may hold the license in its own name for that client’s Licensed Product until it hands the Licensed Product over. The handover is the delivery or acceptance of the Licensed Product that the contract between the contractor and the client names as the handover or, if it names none, the client’s final acceptance of the Licensed Product. Where the contractor works for the client through one or more other contractors, the contracts between them and with the client are read together as the contract between the contractor and the client, a payment for the license to the contractor by the client or by another of those contractors counts as payment by the client, and the client may also name any of those contractors as the new holder. While the contractor holds the license: (a) the Licensed Use and usage limits of the license, and Own Users, the security perimeter, Production Use, End Customers, and the PUBLIC BODIES paragraph, are read as if the client were Developer End User, and delivering the Licensed Product to the client, installing it in the client’s environment, and operating it for the client are not OEM use under Section 17.4; (b) the contractor may deliver the Licensed Product, with its build materials and license keys, to the client or to another contractor that the client names and, where the license includes access to Source Code other than Public Source Code, may also deliver to that recipient that non-public Source Code and Modified Software containing it in source code form that the contractor holds under that license, if the recipient has accepted this EULA or has agreed in writing, with OPTIMAJET or in an undertaking that names OPTIMAJET as entitled to enforce it, to be bound by the CONFIDENTIALITY paragraph of Section 1.3; after that delivery, point (v) of that paragraph does not make the contractor responsible for the acts of that client or contractor; (c) a termination for a breach by the contractor that does not concern that license does not affect it, and on such a termination OPTIMAJET will reissue the license to the client or to another contractor that the client names; and (d) a use of the Licensed Product by the client that goes beyond the license and that the contractor did not make or authorize is the client’s use; the client may cover it under the USE BEYOND THE LICENSE paragraph, counting from the date it receives OPTIMAJET’s notice, the contractor is not responsible for it, and OPTIMAJET may terminate that license under Section 15 if the use is not covered. Until the client accepts this EULA, its employees and other contractors may use the Licensed Product but may not develop with the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) under that license. At the handover, the contractor will assign the license to the client by written notice to OPTIMAJET. If the contractor stops working on the Licensed Product before the handover, it will assign the license in the same way to the client or to another person that the client names for the same Licensed Product, such as the client’s new contractor, provided that, for a license that includes access to Source Code other than Public Source Code, that person does not develop or provide a Competing Product, as and when the contract between the contractor and the client provides or, if that contract is silent, once the client has paid the contractor for the license; a new contractor then holds the license on the same terms, and any other person named holds it as Developer End User. OPTIMAJET will also reissue a license that a contractor holds, to the client or to another person that the client names under the previous sentence, on a joint written notice of the contractor and the client, on a final decision of a court or an arbitral tribunal, or, where the contractor is insolvent or does not answer the client’s written request within thirty (30) days after receiving it, on a written notice of the client with evidence that their contract has ended; OPTIMAJET is not liable for acting on such a notice or decision. OPTIMAJET will reissue the License Statement in the name of the new holder, and move any private source code access to it, at no charge once the new holder accepts this EULA in writing, including through a contract or an order that incorporates it. The reissued license keeps the version of this EULA under which the license was granted unless the new holder chooses the current version in writing, and the license keys issued before the reissue remain valid for the new holder for the same Licensed Product and Licensed Use. A written agreement signed under Section 16 only for that license, and a support plan bought for it, pass to the new holder with the license, together with the contractor’s obligations under them other than paying for them, unless that agreement or plan states otherwise, and OPTIMAJET’s obligations under them to the contractor end at the reissue. From the reissue, the obligations of the contractor for that license end, other than its duty to pay, as they fall due, the amounts that it owes for that license and for such an agreement or plan, its duty not to use that license or its license keys or Source Code other than Public Source Code for any other client or product, not to publish those license keys, and not to give them to anyone other than the new holder and the persons who work on its behalf under this Section 1.1, and the CONFIDENTIALITY paragraph of Section 1.3 for non-public Source Code and the parts of Modified Software that contain it and that the contractor still holds, and it remains liable for its own breaches before the reissue. If the client has not accepted this EULA within ninety (90) days after the handover notice, the obligations of the contractor for the use of the Licensed Product after the handover end when that period ends, but the contractor remains liable for its own breaches; until the client accepts this EULA, OPTIMAJET may decline to renew that license or to issue new license keys for it. A handover notice and an acceptance for a license of Workflow Engine NEO under its end-user license agreement also cover the Form Engine license included in or purchased with it.

USAGE LIMITS. The usage limits that the price list or the License Statement states for a license, such as the number of Domains and wildcard Domains, apply to the license. Within its Licensed Use and the usage limits of its license, including its Domains, a license covers any number of deployments, environments, tenants, users, and instances of the Licensed Product, and adding them needs no further license or fee; a license that covers the copy of one End Customer under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph covers only that copy. A license for OEM use under Section 17.4 also covers the uses under Sections 17.2 and 17.3, and a license for a Public Web Application / SaaS under Section 17.3 also covers internal commercial use under Section 17.2, for the same Licensed Product. Under OEM use, the domains of the installations of End Customers are not Domains of Developer End User and do not count toward its domain limits, and OPTIMAJET will issue license keys for them on request at no charge; it may issue one key that covers the installations of that Licensed Product. A Form Engine license included in or purchased with an edition of Workflow Engine NEO has no limit on Domains unless the price list on workflowengine.io states one.

PUBLIC BODIES. Where Developer End User is a ministry, department, agency, or other public body, its own units, meaning its parts that have no separate legal personality and the bodies that the law makes part of it, are part of Developer End User. What point (ii) of Section 17.5 does not count as use, such as occasional participation of persons of other public bodies in Developer End User’s own internal process, their requests by email, letter, or telephone, and access by auditors, inspectors, law enforcement, and supervisory, legislative, and judicial bodies to review the records of that process, and the work of the individuals of other public bodies whom Section 17.2 makes Own Users, remain internal commercial use. Other public bodies that use a Public Web Application / SaaS of Developer End User in the same way and on the same terms as members of the public, for example by filing their own applications through a portal open to the public, are covered by its license under Section 17.3 and need not be named. Where Developer End User is a public body, any other use of the Licensed Product by other public bodies that are not its Affiliates, including the bodies that act under Developer End User’s direction or supervision by law and the bodies to which Developer End User provides a shared service under a law, a government decision, or a written shared-service arrangement, is covered only by a license that includes OEM use under Section 17.4, whether that use would otherwise count as internal commercial use or as a Public Web Application / SaaS. That license covers only the bodies that the License Statement names or that Developer End User later names in a written notice to OPTIMAJET. Those bodies may use the Licensed Product only through the deployments that Developer End User operates or, where the License Statement says so, through installations of their own. A parent or superior authority of Developer End User may be named like any other body; the license never covers bodies of the same government that are not named, or the government or the state as a whole. Where a contractor holds a license for a public body under the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph, or Developer End User holds a license for the copy of a public body under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph, and that public body pays for its renewal or for a purchase under the USE BEYOND THE LICENSE paragraph through its procurement, the periods that the RENEWAL PRICES, USE BEYOND THE LICENSE, and EARLIER LICENSES paragraphs give a public body apply to that license, and a written commitment that the holder of that license gives OPTIMAJET for that public body under those paragraphs counts as that public body’s commitment.

USE BEYOND THE LICENSE. This paragraph applies if Developer End User’s use goes beyond its license in a way that the license key does not limit, for example in a second product, in a usage type that its license does not include, with the Form Engine Non-commercial License in a commercial product, with the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) used for evaluation in Production Use, with new development or new deployments after an annual subscription ends, or by its contractor for another client. Within thirty (30) days after the earlier of (i) the date its license contact, its legal contact, or a manager responsible for the Licensed Product or for the product in which that use takes place learns of that use and (ii) the date it receives OPTIMAJET’s notice of it, Developer End User may commit in writing to buy the license or upgrade that covers that use, for example by an order, a signed quote, or a purchase request, and then buy it within ninety (90) days after the earlier of those two dates or, for a public body, within the period that its procurement law requires, up to one hundred eighty (180) days. The price is the price in the price list or, for a license whose price the price list does not publish, the price, before any discount, that OPTIMAJET last quoted to Developer End User within the twelve (12) months before the commitment for that license and the same Licensed Product and Licensed Use or, failing that, OPTIMAJET’s standard quote for it, without a surcharge. Where the use is by Developer End User’s contractor for another client, that client may do the same, counting from the date it receives OPTIMAJET’s notice, and the contractor may do the same, counting from the earlier of that date and the date it learns of that use; unless Developer End User authorized that use, OPTIMAJET’s remedies for it lie only against the contractor and the other client, despite the PEOPLE paragraph, and the license of Developer End User is not terminated for it. Once the license or upgrade is bought, that use is licensed from its start and is not a breach of this EULA. A license or upgrade bought under this paragraph starts on the date that use started, and for an annual subscription Developer End User will pay for each term that has started since then. If Developer End User commits under this paragraph and does not buy within that period, the price is a sum due to OPTIMAJET. If, within those thirty (30) days, Developer End User disputes in writing and in good faith, stating its reasons, that the use goes beyond its license, its continued use while the dispute is pending is not an intentional breach, and OPTIMAJET will not terminate the license for that use while the dispute is pending. The dispute stops being pending when Developer End User withdraws it; when ninety (90) days have passed after OPTIMAJET rejects it in writing and neither party has started arbitration or court proceedings about it under the GOVERNING LAW AND DISPUTES paragraph of Section 16; or when proceedings so started end without a decision on it. If the dispute is resolved against Developer End User, by agreement or by a final decision, or stops being pending, the periods for the commitment and the purchase under this paragraph run from that date, and the license or upgrade covers the whole period of that use. This paragraph does not apply to a breach of Section 1.2, of the CONFIDENTIALITY paragraph of Section 1.3, or of point (a) or (c) of Section 1.4.

EVALUATION. Developer End User may use Form Engine Designer and the rest of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) free of charge and without functional limits, to evaluate them, to develop and test a future Licensed Product, including a commercial one, and to demonstrate it to prospective customers in Non-Production Use, before it buys a license for that Licensed Product. An integrator or another contractor may do the same to develop a future Licensed Product for a client before the client’s license is bought. After an annual subscription for a Licensed Product ends, new development of that Licensed Product with the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) requires a renewal. A commercial license that includes the packages used is needed before Production Use, except for the non-commercial use described in Section 17.1.

UPDATES AND PERPETUAL USE. A perpetual license does not expire. It covers the versions of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) that OPTIMAJET releases during the update period stated in the price list on the date of purchase or in the License Statement, including major versions, and during each renewal of that period. When the update period ends, Developer End User may keep using the versions released during the update period and its renewals, within the Licensed Use, without time limit. New versions released after that require a renewal of updates at the price that the price list states (or, if it states none, at the price that OPTIMAJET quotes for that license), or on the terms of the quote or agreement for that license. A license sold as an annual subscription is granted for the term paid. When the term ends, the license continues only for the copies of the Licensed Product then in use, including their Non-Production Use, without updates (except where mandatory law requires OPTIMAJET to provide security updates free of charge), new development, or new deployments; new development and new deployments with the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) require a renewal. Until the subscription is renewed, Form Engine Designer, including Form Engine Designer delivered as part of a Licensed Product, shows a notice of non-commercial use, which must remain fully visible, except in a copy that runs with the license key of a perpetual license that an End Customer has bought directly from OPTIMAJET; OPTIMAJET will issue that key for that copy on request. A new release of the Licensed Product that contains Form Engine Designer is new development, and installing the Licensed Product for a new End Customer or in a new production environment, or giving a new End Customer access to a hosted Licensed Product, including as a new tenant, is a new deployment; restarting, scaling, or restoring after a failure, including in a replacement environment, the copies then in use for the End Customers that used them when the subscription ended is neither. An End Customer of a Licensed Product under OEM use may buy from OPTIMAJET, at the price in the price list, a perpetual license for the copy that it uses, of a type listed in Section 18 whose Licensed Use, Domains, and features cover its own use of that copy. A license sold as perpetual and paid in installments becomes perpetual when it is paid in full; until then, it runs only for the period paid, and OPTIMAJET may issue its license keys for that period only.

RENEWAL PRICES. OPTIMAJET will announce a new renewal price by notice to the license contact at least ninety (90) days before it applies. A renewal price fixed for a prepaid period does not change during that period. The renewal price of an existing license, for updates or for an annual subscription, may increase only once in any period of twelve (12) months. The renewal price per year may exceed the price per year of the previous renewal of that license before any discount by no more than ten percent (10%) for each year, or period of three hundred sixty-five (365) days, that the previous renewal covered; for a first renewal, it may exceed the renewal price per year that the price list or the quote stated on the date of purchase by no more than ten percent (10%) for each year, or period of three hundred sixty-five (365) days, of the first update period or term. A discount that OPTIMAJET gives for a purchase or a renewal, including for a prepaid period, applies to that purchase or renewal only, unless its quote states otherwise. This paragraph applies to a renewal made before the period it renews ends or, for a public body that commits in writing to the renewal before that period ends, for example by a notice of its intent to renew, which may be subject to the availability of funds and to its procurement procedure, within the period that its procurement law requires, up to one hundred eighty (180) days after it ends; the renewed period then starts on the day after the previous period ended, and a public body’s renewal made after that day is backdated to it. Any other renewal made after the period it renews ends is priced as the price list states. This paragraph applies to a renewal of the same scope (the same license, Licensed Use, Licensed Product, and Domains, whatever the length of the renewed period) and prevails over the price list and over a quote for it. New purchases follow the price list. No license, update period, Source Code Window, or subscription renews automatically unless Developer End User chooses automatic renewal when it buys. OPTIMAJET will remind the license contact in writing at least thirty (30) days before an automatic renewal and before the end of an annual subscription, an update period, or a period paid in installments, and Developer End User may cancel an automatic renewal at any time before its date.

EARLIER LICENSES. A license bought under a license type that OPTIMAJET no longer sells, including the Form Engine Custom License (issued as the Formengine Custom License), keeps the terms of its purchase until it is renewed under the NEW VERSIONS paragraph of Section 16; a license for a term, including an annual subscription, runs until the end of the term paid, and a perpetual license keeps the right to use the versions released during its update periods without time limit. A renewal continues the same scope or moves to the closest current license that is not smaller; a Form Engine Custom License with OEM use may move to Form Engine Enterprise. A move to another license is priced as the price list or OPTIMAJET’s quote for that move states, and the RENEWAL PRICES paragraph applies from its next renewal. OPTIMAJET will announce on formengine.io, at least twelve (12) months in advance, the date after which it stops renewing updates of an earlier license, and will notify the license contacts of that license of that date; a renewal made before that date may move to the closest current license that is not smaller. After that date, or where the update period of a license has ended before the renewal and the renewal is not a public body’s renewal within the period that the RENEWAL PRICES paragraph allows, OPTIMAJET may decline to renew updates of that license; Developer End User may then buy any current license.

CHANGES TO LICENSES. A later change of the names, composition, or prices of OPTIMAJET’s licenses does not reduce the rights under a license already purchased for the versions released before the change and during the update period in which the change takes effect.

LICENSE INFORMATION. Developer End User must promptly provide OPTIMAJET, directly or through the purchaser acting on its behalf, with the information reasonably necessary to prepare and maintain an accurate License Statement for a purchased license it holds. That information must be true, accurate, complete, and current, including, as applicable, Developer End User’s legal name and business address, the identity of the purchaser and any End Customer whose copy the license covers, the name and primary purpose of the Licensed Product, its intended Licensed Use, and the license contact’s business contact details. Developer End User must promptly notify OPTIMAJET in writing of any change to, or error in, that information. A separate legal contact or security contact is optional. Providing or correcting information does not by itself expand the purchased license or authorize a transfer. Requests to identify Licensed Products remain subject to the frequency limit, response period, and standard of knowledge in the LICENSE KEYS AND DATA paragraph. The restrictions on access to systems and data in that paragraph also remain unchanged.

LICENSE KEYS AND DATA. The parts of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) that Developer End User installs verify license keys and domain names locally and work without any connection to systems operated by OPTIMAJET. They do not send OPTIMAJET any data about Developer End User, its Licensed Products, their users, or the data they process; OPTIMAJET’s websites, online documentation, and hosted demos are governed by its privacy notice. If a later version offers a feature that sends data to OPTIMAJET, that feature will be off unless Developer End User turns it on. Developer End User may choose to give OPTIMAJET details of an installation, for example to receive support; the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) does not send them by itself. OPTIMAJET will use the confidential information of Developer End User only to issue, verify, and administer licenses, renewals, and payments, to send the notices and advisories that this EULA provides for, to register projects, to provide support, and, in a form that identifies neither Developer End User nor its End Customers, to improve its products and services, which does not include copying Developer End User’s code or forms into OPTIMAJET’s products or using them to train machine-learning models. OPTIMAJET will protect that confidential information with at least reasonable care. OPTIMAJET will disclose that information only to its personnel and contractors who need it for those purposes and are bound by confidentiality. OPTIMAJET will delete that information on request once it is no longer needed for those purposes. OPTIMAJET may also use and disclose that information in confidence to enforce or defend its rights under this EULA, and may disclose it to the extent that a law, a court, or a public authority with jurisdiction over OPTIMAJET requires, after notifying Developer End User, where the law permits, so that Developer End User can seek protection; it may keep that information for as long as a law or a litigation hold requires. This EULA gives OPTIMAJET no access to the systems of Developer End User or to personal data or government data other than the business contact details of the persons who request, buy, or manage a license, and OPTIMAJET does not need or ask for such access or data to issue licenses, license keys, and updates or to provide support. Developer End User may remove such data from anything that it sends to OPTIMAJET and will not send it protected health information or other data whose processing requires a business associate agreement or terms beyond the data processing terms that OPTIMAJET agrees to under this paragraph, or government-related data or bulk U.S. sensitive personal data as 28 C.F.R. Part 202 defines them. If personal data or government data, other than the business contact details described above, reaches OPTIMAJET and is not data that this paragraph prohibits Developer End User from sending, OPTIMAJET will, to the extent permitted by applicable law and any data processing terms agreed under this paragraph, use it only to answer the request with which it was sent. If data that this paragraph prohibits Developer End User from sending reaches OPTIMAJET, OPTIMAJET will not use it to answer a request and may handle it only as permitted by applicable law to isolate, return, or delete it, or as required by applicable law. For personal data or government data, other than the business contact details described above, that reaches OPTIMAJET, whether or not this paragraph prohibits Developer End User from sending it, OPTIMAJET will not sell it or disclose it other than as the previous sentences permit, will delete it within ninety (90) days after the request is resolved or, if Developer End User so requests, without further use and without undue delay, other than copies in backups, which are deleted in their normal cycle, unless a law or a litigation hold requires OPTIMAJET to keep it, and will confirm the deletion in writing if Developer End User so requests. OPTIMAJET will notify Developer End User in writing without undue delay after it becomes aware of any unauthorized access to, or disclosure of, Developer End User’s confidential information held by OPTIMAJET, or of any accidental or unlawful destruction, loss, or alteration of personal data that it processes on behalf of Developer End User. Where OPTIMAJET nevertheless processes personal data on behalf of Developer End User, for example in material sent for support, OPTIMAJET will, on request, agree on data processing terms with Developer End User that meet the law that applies to that processing. OPTIMAJET may ask Developer End User in writing, not more than once in any period of twelve (12) months, to name the Licensed Products in which it uses the license keys that OPTIMAJET issued to it or for a license that it holds, and Developer End User will name them in writing, to its knowledge after reasonable inquiry, within thirty (30) days after the request.

## 1.2 COMPETING PRODUCTS

This Section 1.2 applies to every license granted under this EULA. Publishing or maintaining a source-only fork or patch that complies with the PUBLIC SOURCE CODE paragraph of Section 1.3 is not, by itself, developing or providing a Competing Product. This exception does not cover offering a separate product, SDK, or service for third parties to use as a substitute for the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S).

DEFINITION. “Competing Product” means software, a library, a component, a hosted service, or a platform, whether paid or free, whose primary purpose is to let third parties design, build, or render forms in software that they develop themselves. It includes:

(a) a form builder, form designer, or form rendering engine offered as a library, component, SDK, or standalone tool;

(b) an application programming interface, SDK, or hosted service that gives third-party developers substantially the same capabilities as the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S); and

(c) a product marketed as a substitute for, or an alternative to, the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S).

The points above are examples; software falls under them only if its primary purpose is the one stated in this definition. In this Section, third parties do not include Developer End User, its Affiliates, the Authorized Contractors working for them, or the public bodies named under the PUBLIC BODIES paragraph of Section 1.1; an internal platform used only by them is not a Competing Product, and each separate product built on it needs its own license where the LICENSED USE paragraph of Section 1.1 requires one.

RESTRICTION. The licenses granted in this EULA do not extend to any use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S), the Source Code, Modified Software, or OPTIMAJET’s confidential information in a Competing Product, for a Competing Product, or to develop one. Developer End User may not make such use and may not provide any of those items to anyone for that purpose.

WHAT IS NOT A COMPETING PRODUCT. A Licensed Product is not a Competing Product merely because:

(i) it uses the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) to provide forms that are part of its own functionality, in any industry, for example healthcare, banking, insurance, supply chain, or HR;

(ii) its End Customers create or change forms through the user interface or the APIs of the Licensed Product, including the embedded Form Engine Designer, provided that they do not receive the programming interfaces of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) to develop their own software outside the Licensed Product, other than the interfaces that Section 6 permits;

(iii) it is a low-code, workflow, or business process management application in which End Customers configure their forms and processes through the Licensed Product, within the limits of point (ii); or

(iv) it offers APIs that let End Customers use the Licensed Product or integrate it with their other systems, including APIs that create or submit forms.

TIME OF ASSESSMENT. Whether a Licensed Product is a Competing Product is decided by its primary purpose when Developer End User first makes it available to third parties and, if Developer End User later changes that primary purpose, again at that time. A Licensed Product does not become a Competing Product later because OPTIMAJET releases a new product, edition, or capability.

INDEPENDENT DEVELOPMENT. Nothing in this EULA restricts Developer End User from developing any software, including software that competes with OPTIMAJET’s products, independently and without use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) or OPTIMAJET’s confidential information. For this Section 1.2, developing software with an AI coding assistant, code index, or cache that holds the Source Code or code decompiled from the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) in a project, workspace, or other development context that Developer End User supplies, selects, or connects for that development is a use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S); once that code is removed from those contexts, later development with those tools is not, for that reason alone, such a use. A provider’s independent storage of Public Source Code outside those contexts does not by itself make Developer End User’s development a use of that code. This exception does not permit Developer End User to supply, retrieve, or use that code for a Competing Product, or to direct a service to do so. Form Engine Core may be used under the MIT License for any purpose that license allows.

CONFIRMATION. Developer End User may describe a product to OPTIMAJET in writing and ask whether it is a Competing Product. OPTIMAJET will answer in writing within fifteen (15) business days. If OPTIMAJET does not answer in time, its silence is not a confirmation. A written confirmation that a product is not a Competing Product and that states that it is given under this paragraph binds OPTIMAJET for that product as described and for its later versions with the same primary purpose. OPTIMAJET’s confirmations and any guidance it publishes on this Section 1.2 can only narrow the scope of this Section, never widen it. Where a License Statement, or a quote that Developer End User accepted in writing, describes the primary purpose of the Licensed Product, OPTIMAJET confirms by issuing the license that a product with that primary purpose, as described, is not a Competing Product; the name of a product alone is not such a description.

## 1.3 SOURCE CODE AND MODIFICATIONS

This Section 1.3 applies to Public Source Code and, where Developer End User’s license includes such access, to other Source Code. Public availability is not a grant of unrestricted commercial use or an open-source license. The MIT License and third-party licenses remain unaffected as the second paragraph of this EULA and Section 7 provide.

PUBLIC SOURCE CODE. Subject to this EULA, Developer End User may, without buying a license, access, copy, study, audit, modify, and build Public Source Code for the evaluation, development, testing, and demonstration permitted by the EVALUATION paragraph of Section 1.1, including for a future commercial Licensed Product, and for the non-commercial use permitted by Section 17.1. A paid license is required before any Production Use that Section 17 requires to be commercially licensed. These free rights do not renew an expired subscription or permit new development or new deployments of its Licensed Product where Section 1.1 requires renewal.

Developer End User may also publish and share Public Source Code and Modifications to it as source-only forks, patches, or pull requests, including repository-generated source archives, provided that it: (i) includes this EULA in the version under which OPTIMAJET published that code, all applicable license and copyright notices, and a clear notice that changes are unofficial; (ii) grants recipients the rights in its Modifications needed to exercise the same rights, subject to the same conditions, under that version of this EULA, without representing that OPTIMAJET authored, approved, supports, or warrants those Modifications; (iii) includes no non-public Source Code, confidential information, or license key, except an example key that OPTIMAJET expressly publishes for redistribution; and (iv) complies with Sections 1.2 and 1.4. Recipients must comply with this EULA for OPTIMAJET’s code and need their own paid license before any Production Use for which Section 17 requires a commercial license; a fork does not transfer the publisher’s commercial license, Domains, update entitlement, or license keys. This permission applies despite the restrictions on sharing and distribution in Sections 5, 6, and 17.1. Merely maintaining or publishing a permitted fork is not commercial Production Use and does not itself require a paid license.

This source-sharing permission does not permit public distribution of standalone ready-to-use builds or distributions of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) or Modified Software, including built, bundled, or minified npm packages, container images, binary releases, or downloadable CI build artifacts. Ordinary human-readable source files and build scripts remain source files for this purpose even if their programming language allows them to run without compilation. OPTIMAJET alone publishes such standalone public distributions, unless it authorizes another publisher in writing. Private builds, caches, package registries, and CI artifacts used by Developer End User and the persons working on its behalf for a use permitted by this EULA are allowed. Obtaining source through a version-control dependency and building it for such a use is allowed. Delivery of built files within a Licensed Product, including to browsers, OEM distribution, and evaluation builds, remains governed by Section 6. A source archive may include the build scripts and supporting source materials that OPTIMAJET publishes with the corresponding source release. This does not authorize redistributing a standalone ready-to-use build merely because a source archive also contains it.

Public Source Code must not be used to train or fine-tune machine-learning models. AI coding assistants may be used with it under terms or settings under which the provider does not use it for training and does not disclose it beyond what is needed to provide the service. These restrictions do not restrict a right that applicable law does not allow OPTIMAJET to restrict. Nothing in this EULA withdraws rights that OPTIMAJET separately grants, through its authorized publication of Public Source Code and the applicable platform agreement, to the hosting platform or its users. This recognition grants no additional right under this EULA to commercial Production Use, unpaid updates, standalone built distribution, or bypassing Protected Code.

LICENSE. Subject to all the terms and conditions of this EULA, OPTIMAJET grants Developer End User a non-exclusive, worldwide license, not transferable except as Section 5 and the CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraph of Section 1.1 permit, to:

(a) use, copy, review, audit, modify, and compile the Source Code to develop, test, maintain, and support the Licensed Product of that license, and to review and audit the Source Code, including in a debugger, for the other Licensed Products of Developer End User;

(b) use Modified Software as part of the Licensed Product of that license within its Licensed Use, including by delivering its client-side files in built form to the browsers of End Customers under Section 6; and

(c) where the Licensed Use includes OEM use under Section 17.4, distribute Modified Software in built (compiled, bundled, or minified) form only, as an integral part of the Licensed Product, under Section 6.

VERSIONS AND DURATION. For Public Source Code, the paid rights under the LICENSE paragraph cover only the versions covered by Developer End User’s paid license under Section 1.1. Public access to a later version, a fork, or a build does not add it to that entitlement. Incorporating code from a later OPTIMAJET release into an older version requires entitlement to that later code for use under the paid license; changing a version number, release date, or build date does not change this. Independently created Modifications do not become a later OPTIMAJET release merely because they are made or built later. Corrections and security updates that this EULA or mandatory law requires OPTIMAJET to provide remain covered as those provisions require. For other Source Code, the LICENSE paragraph covers every version that OPTIMAJET makes available to Developer End User during its Source Code Window. For a perpetual license, Developer End User may keep using, modifying, building, and, within its Licensed Use, distributing its covered versions without time limit after that window ends. For an annual subscription, the limits after its term ends in Section 1.1 continue to apply. The paid rights never last longer than the paid license; the separate free rights to Public Source Code remain subject to the PUBLIC SOURCE CODE paragraph and Section 15.

OWNERSHIP. OPTIMAJET and the respective rights holders retain their rights in the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) and the Source Code, including the parts of them contained in Modified Software. OPTIMAJET’s rights in contributions received under a contributor license agreement are the rights granted by that agreement; their inclusion in the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) does not by itself transfer copyright ownership to OPTIMAJET. As between OPTIMAJET and Developer End User, and subject to those rights, Developer End User owns its Modifications and its Licensed Product; their ownership as between Developer End User and its contractors is governed by their agreement. Except as the CONTRIBUTIONS paragraph of this Section provides, nothing in this EULA gives OPTIMAJET any right to a Licensed Product or to Developer End User’s own code, and Developer End User is not required to share its Modifications with OPTIMAJET.

CONTRIBUTIONS. A contribution intentionally submitted to OPTIMAJET through its repository contribution process is governed by the contributor license agreement that the contributor separately accepts for that contribution. That agreement governs the rights granted in the contribution and prevails over this paragraph for those rights; it does not replace the commercial license needed to use the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S). No change to that agreement binds an earlier contribution without the required acceptance. The existing Form Engine contributor license agreement grants copyright and patent licenses and permits commercial relicensing; it does not assign copyright ownership to OPTIMAJET. A contributor may grant only rights that it owns or is authorized to grant, including any rights of its employer. Where no separately accepted contributor agreement applies, Developer End User’s submission of Modifications, fixes, or suggestions expressly designated as a contribution, or of its correction required by Section 1.4, is subject to the following grant: Developer End User grants OPTIMAJET a perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide, non-exclusive, royalty-free license to use, copy, modify, sublicense, and distribute that contribution as part of OPTIMAJET’s products, including under commercial licenses, and will not assert its copyright in that contribution against OPTIMAJET or its customers with respect to those products. Information sent for support, a privately maintained Modification, or publication of a fork or patch without submission to OPTIMAJET is not a contribution merely for that reason. OPTIMAJET remains free to develop functionality independently.

CONFIDENTIALITY. This paragraph applies only to Source Code other than Public Source Code, and to the parts of Modified Software that contain such non-public Source Code. References to Source Code in this paragraph mean that non-public code. It is OPTIMAJET’s confidential information and, to the extent it qualifies under applicable law, its trade secret. Developer End User will:

(i) use the Source Code only as this Section 1.3 permits;

(ii) except for the built client-side files delivered to browsers under Section 6, give access to the Source Code, and to Modified Software in source code form, only to its employees, the employees of its Affiliates, and its Authorized Contractors who need it for a Licensed Product and who are bound by written obligations that prohibit them from disclosing it and from using it for any other purpose (an AI coding assistant or an online service used under point (iii) is not a person given access);

(iii) keep the Source Code in access-controlled systems, never use it to train or fine-tune a model, and never publish it or upload it to a public code repository, a public package registry, or an online service that uses it to train models or discloses it to anyone other than as needed to provide the service, except that Developer End User may use AI coding assistants with the Source Code if they are used under terms or settings under which the provider does not use the Source Code to train models and does not disclose it to anyone other than as needed to provide the service;

(iv) notify OPTIMAJET in writing without undue delay after it becomes aware of any unauthorized access to, or disclosure of, the Source Code;

(v) be responsible for the acts and omissions of the persons to whom it gives access as for its own;

(vi) when a person no longer needs access, end that access and make sure that the person returns or deletes the copies of the Source Code that it holds; and

(vii) after an unauthorized disclosure, take the steps reasonably available to it to remove the Source Code from public access, keep the evidence of the disclosure, and cooperate with OPTIMAJET’s requests to take it down.

Developer End User may disclose the Source Code to the extent that a law, a court, or a supervisory authority with jurisdiction over it requires, after notifying OPTIMAJET where the law permits and asking for confidential treatment, and may give access to its auditors, its regulators and other supervisory authorities, and its legal advisers who are bound by confidentiality. Information that is or becomes public other than through a breach of a confidentiality obligation owed to OPTIMAJET is not confidential. The Source Code does not become Public Source Code because it is disclosed without OPTIMAJET’s authorization, and the CONFIDENTIALITY paragraph of this Section continues to apply to it.

These obligations continue after the Source Code Window and this EULA end.

RESPONSIBILITY FOR MODIFICATIONS. Modifications and Modified Software are the sole responsibility of Developer End User. OPTIMAJET’s support and the warranty in Section 12 cover only the officially published versions of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S), and, except as a support agreement with OPTIMAJET provides, OPTIMAJET corrects defects and security vulnerabilities only in the current version. Developer End User is responsible for correcting them in its Modified Software and, except where mandatory law requires OPTIMAJET to provide security updates free of charge, in the versions it keeps after the Source Code Window ends, or may renew the Source Code Window to receive the current version with its corrections and its Source Code. To get support for an issue in Modified Software, Developer End User must either reproduce it on an officially published version or request a quote.

COMPLIANCE CONFIRMATION. OPTIMAJET may ask Developer End User in writing, not more than once in any period of twelve (12) months, to confirm in writing that its use of the Source Code complies with this EULA, and Developer End User will, within thirty (30) days after the request, confirm in writing, to its knowledge after reasonable inquiry, that its use of the Source Code complies with this EULA or describe how it does not. No confirmation is required without such a request.

## 1.4 LICENSE KEYS AND PROTECTED CODE

“Protected Code” means the parts of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) and of the Source Code that verify license keys or Domains, enforce the license type, capabilities, usage limits, or domain limits of a license, or display the notices or warnings that the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) shows when it runs without a valid license key or after an annual subscription ends; the data that the verification of license keys and Domains uses; the marks that identify the license under which OPTIMAJET made a copy of the Source Code available; and every part that OPTIMAJET identifies as Protected Code in the Source Code or in its documentation. An identification applies only to the versions of the Source Code in which OPTIMAJET makes it.

Developer End User may not, and may not permit anyone to:

(a) change, disable, remove, bypass, or circumvent the Protected Code or the license key functionality, including by hiding or visually overlapping a notice or warning that it displays;

(b) use the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) or Modified Software to obtain licenses, capabilities, versions, products, or uses that its license does not cover, or to exceed the usage or domain limits of its license;

(c) create, use, or distribute license keys that OPTIMAJET did not issue; or

(d) use a license key outside the purpose for which OPTIMAJET issued it, including outside its Licensed Product and its Licensed Use, or in Production Use outside its Domains, or share it with anyone for such use.

Public availability of Protected Code permits its inspection under Section 1.3; it does not authorize any act prohibited by this Section. The licenses granted in this EULA do not extend to any use described in points (a) through (d). A use for which a license or upgrade is bought under the USE BEYOND THE LICENSE paragraph of Section 1.1, within the periods stated there, by Developer End User or by the other client or the contractor referred to there, is not a breach of point (b) or (d). A change to the Source Code that does not change which licenses, capabilities, versions, uses, Domains, or limits the license key permits, and does not remove or hide a notice or warning that the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) shows when it runs without a valid license key or after an annual subscription ends, or a mark that identifies the license, is not a breach of point (a).

Developer End User may not publish a license key, other than inside a Licensed Product as Section 6 permits, in the files delivered to browsers under Section 6, or where OPTIMAJET expressly publishes that example key for redistribution. Developer End User will tell OPTIMAJET without undue delay after it learns that a key has been published or used without authorization. Permitted redistribution of an example key does not by itself require notice or replacement and does not expand the purpose or use for which OPTIMAJET issued it. After such unauthorized publication or use, OPTIMAJET will issue a replacement key at no charge, and versions released after the replacement may refuse the published key.

Building a covered version of the Source Code with Modifications, including after the Source Code Window ends, is not a change of the Protected Code if the build keeps the original version identity, release date, and other data that the license key verification uses for that version, and OPTIMAJET will provide the build setting or the license key that such a build needs. Developer End User will send to OPTIMAJET any correction of a security vulnerability that it makes in the Protected Code, to the extent that the export control and sanctions laws that apply to Developer End User permit it, and that correction is a contribution under the CONTRIBUTIONS paragraph of Section 1.3.

OPTIMAJET will issue the license keys that a Licensed Product needs within the Licensed Use, the Domains, and the features that the license permits, including keys for its desktop editions, so that Developer End User never needs to change the Protected Code. OPTIMAJET will issue a license key that the license permits, including a key for a changed Domain or for the domain of a standby system, at no charge within two (2) business days after Developer End User requests it by email to hello@optimajet.com; for a renewal or an installment whose payment OPTIMAJET receives at least two (2) business days before the end of the period that it continues, OPTIMAJET will issue the new license key before that period ends. This paragraph is subject to the PAYMENT and CONTRACTOR-HELD LICENSES paragraphs of Section 1.1 and to Section 11.

## 2. LIMITATIONS ON REVERSE ENGINEERING, DECOMPILATION, AND DISASSEMBLY

