DISCONTINUATION. OPTIMAJET will announce on formengine.io at least twelve (12) months in advance that it will stop offering new licenses of Form Engine Designer. At the time of that announcement, OPTIMAJET will also send a copy by email, in accordance with the NOTICES paragraph of this Section, to the license contact and any legal contact on record for each paid license of the affected product whose update period is then current. Perpetual licenses already granted for that product continue, and Developer End User may keep using the versions released during their update periods within the Licensed Use. An annual subscription of that product in force on the date of the announcement may be renewed until the date that the announcement names and, after the end of its last term paid, continues as the UPDATES AND PERPETUAL USE paragraph of Section 1.1 states.

## 17. USAGE TYPES FOR PRODUCT LICENSING

### 17.1. Non-Commercial Use

Non-commercial use is the use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) exclusively for personal, educational, scientific, or research purposes, without obtaining any direct or indirect financial gain. Except for sharing Public Source Code under Section 1.3, this use does not include sharing the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) with third parties or distributing it to them, developing or providing services based on it for third parties, or any commercial activity associated with the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S).

### 17.1.1. Examples of Non-Commercial Use

- Personal projects: using the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) to develop and test personal projects that are not related to any commercial activities.
- Academic and research purposes: using the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) as part of educational programs, scientific research, or academic projects, with no intention of generating commercial profit.

### 17.1.2. Conditions for Transition to a Commercial License

The EVALUATION paragraph of Section 1.1 and Section 6 set out free evaluation and development and when a commercial license is required.

### 17.1.3. Additional Clarification

Production Use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) in the internal processes of an organization, including a public body or a non-profit organization, other than for the purposes of Section 17.1, and Production Use of a product or service intended for commercial sale or monetization, do not qualify as non-commercial use and require a commercial license, except as the UPDATES AND PERPETUAL USE paragraph of Section 1.1 permits after an annual subscription ends.

### 17.2. Internal Commercial Use

Internal commercial use is the use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) in a Licensed Product that only Own Users use, in any number of deployments, on the Domains that the license permits, inside the security perimeter of Developer End User and its Affiliates. “Own Users” are the employees of Developer End User and of its Affiliates and the individuals who work for them under a written agreement, directly or through a contractor, such as consultants and outsourced or temporary staff, while they act for Developer End User or its Affiliates. Customers of Developer End User or its Affiliates, the dealers, distributors, resellers, and franchisees that sell or distribute their products or services, public bodies other than Developer End User and its Affiliates, and the individuals who work for any of them are not Own Users, even where an agreement says that they act for Developer End User or its Affiliates. Agents and brokers that run their own agency or brokerage, and the individuals who work for them, are not Own Users either. However, the individuals of a public body, or of a company that a public body owns, that hosts, operates, develops, or supports the Licensed Product for Developer End User or its Affiliates under a law, a government decision, or a written agreement, and the individuals that another public body seconds to Developer End User or its Affiliates, are Own Users while they do that work for them, but not when they use the Licensed Product in the processes of their own body. A deployment is inside the security perimeter if no person other than Own Users uses it and it runs in an environment of Developer End User, of its Affiliates, or of a hosting provider or another contractor that runs it for them, including cloud environments operated for them and the computers and mobile devices that Own Users use to work for them, even where Own Users reach the deployment over the internet. Section 17.5 sets out how to determine the usage type, including which participation of other persons does not count as their use, and Section 17.6 gives examples.

### 17.3. Public Web Application / SaaS

Public Web Application / SaaS use is the use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) in one Licensed Product that is a SaaS application or a public web application: a software solution that persons other than Own Users use and that Developer End User or its Affiliates operate themselves or through a hosting provider or another contractor on their behalf, including a service that partners offer under their own brands. Its user interface may be a web, desktop, or mobile application that connects to the service, in versions for various platforms (for example, iOS and Android versions of a mobile app); an installation in an environment that an End Customer owns or controls, such as its own data center or a cloud account held in its name, is OEM use under Section 17.4, even where Developer End User operates it, unless a license for that copy is held under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph of Section 1.1. An instance in an environment of Developer End User or its Affiliates, or of a hosting provider on their behalf, remains Public Web Application / SaaS use even where its End Customer has administrative access to it. The SaaS application is named in the License Statement. It may serve any number of tenants and users, in any number of instances that Developer End User or its Affiliates operate themselves or through a hosting provider on their behalf, including instances dedicated to individual End Customers, on the Domains that the license permits.

### 17.4. OEM Use

OEM use is the use of the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) as an integral part of a Licensed Product provided to third parties: distribution, by Developer End User or its Affiliates, to any number of End Customers, in any number of installations, with no royalties. A copy delivered to End Customers, for example as an installer, a container image, a virtual machine, a device, or a desktop or mobile application that works without a service that Developer End User or its Affiliates operate themselves or through a hosting provider or another contractor on their behalf, is OEM use. An installation of the Licensed Product in an environment that an End Customer owns or controls, such as its own data center or a cloud account held in its name, is OEM use, even where Developer End User operates it, unless a license for that copy is held under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph of Section 1.1. An instance in an environment of Developer End User or its Affiliates, or of a hosting provider on their behalf, remains Public Web Application / SaaS use under Section 17.3 even where its End Customer has administrative access to it. Form Engine Enterprise (Section 18), including the Form Engine license that comes with Workflow Engine NEO Enterprise, includes OEM use.

### 17.5. How to Determine the Usage Type

Developer End User determines the usage type that a Licensed Product needs by answering three questions, in this order: (1) does any person other than Own Users use it, or does any copy run in an environment that a client or another third party owns or controls, other than a hosting provider or another contractor that runs it only for Developer End User or its Affiliates and is not an End Customer? If neither, internal commercial use under Section 17.2 covers it; (2) if so, does every copy of it run in an environment of Developer End User, of its Affiliates, or of a hosting provider or another contractor, other than an End Customer, that runs it on their behalf? If so, it needs Public Web Application / SaaS use under Section 17.3; (3) if any copy runs in an environment of an End Customer or is delivered to End Customers, it needs OEM use under Section 17.4. The usage type depends only on who uses the Licensed Product and where the copies that serve those users run, and not on how Developer End User prices, sells, or brands it. For these questions:

(i) A person uses the Licensed Product if the person works in its user interface, including through a link, or submits or tracks the person’s own requests in it, such as applications, claims, orders, or tickets, or answers questions in them, directly or through a website, a mobile application, a chat bot, an API, or another interface that Developer End User or its Affiliates provide to such persons, including one that passes the requests on by email or as files. Persons who work in a Licensed Product regularly, such as through a portal for customers, suppliers, dealers, or partners, use it, and so do persons who submit their own requests, even if each of them does so only occasionally.

(ii) Despite point (i), the following are not use by persons other than Own Users: (A) occasional participation in Developer End User’s own internal process that Own Users carry out, for example a supplier or a customer who, through a link that an Own User sends for a step that the Own User decides to ask of that person, and not a link that the Licensed Product sends by itself as a step of the request, confirms or approves that step, fills in a form, or provides documents or information that Own Users ask for, including in that person’s own request, where the link does not let the person submit new requests or track them; (B) documents that suppliers or other business partners send in Developer End User’s own purchasing or administrative process, such as invoices, bids, or delivery confirmations, but not orders that customers or resellers of Developer End User or its Affiliates place, and not documents that any of those suppliers, business partners, customers, or resellers send through a portal or another user interface of the Licensed Product that they use regularly; (C) notifications, letters, documents, and other results of a process that persons receive, including through a link that only shows or downloads that result as it stood when the link was sent, without otherwise using the Licensed Product; (D) requests that persons send in their own email, text, or chat messages or letters, or make by telephone, and that Own Users review and handle in the Licensed Product, even where it imports those messages automatically; (E) work that Own Users do in the Licensed Product to provide the services of Developer End User or its Affiliates to their clients, where the clients do not use it themselves; and (F) access by auditors, inspectors, law enforcement, and supervisory, legislative, and judicial bodies to review records. A chat bot, an email address, a file transfer, or another channel through which the Licensed Product completes requests, or answers questions about their status, without an Own User reviewing each of them, is an interface under point (i).

(iii) Files of the Licensed Product delivered to the browsers of its users, and the desktop and mobile applications of the Licensed Product that its users receive to use the Licensed Product as a hosted service, are part of the Public Web Application / SaaS, and not OEM use, while they work only with that hosted service, which Developer End User or its Affiliates operate themselves or through a hosting provider or another contractor on their behalf, even where those files and applications contain the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S); a version that works without that service, such as one installed on an End Customer’s servers or an offline desktop application, is OEM use.

(iv) A deployment in an environment of an Affiliate for the use of Own Users is internal commercial use.

(v) Despite point (i), a separate Public Web Application / SaaS, or another separate product, of Developer End User or of its Affiliate through which persons other than Own Users use a Licensed Product needs its own license under Section 17.3, and the Licensed Product keeps its own usage type, as the LICENSED USE paragraph of Section 1.1 provides.

(vi) A license for OEM use also covers the uses under Sections 17.2 and 17.3, and a license for a Public Web Application / SaaS also covers internal commercial use, for the same Licensed Product, as the USAGE LIMITS paragraph of Section 1.1 provides; a Licensed Product used in more than one of these ways needs the highest of them.

(vii) Where Developer End User is a public body, the PUBLIC BODIES paragraph of Section 1.1 governs the use by other public bodies, and the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph of Section 1.1 governs licenses for the copies of individual End Customers.

The examples in Section 17.6 are part of this EULA, and Developer End User may ask OPTIMAJET in writing which usage type an application needs, as the LICENSED USE paragraph of Section 1.1 provides.

### 17.6. Examples

The following examples are part of this EULA and assume that the Licensed Product stays within the usage limits of its license, including its Domains:

(1) Employees of a company and of its Affiliates design and fill in forms in an internal application on the Domains of the license, including from home over the internet: internal commercial use.

(2) In the company’s own purchasing process, a supplier fills in a delivery form through a link in an email: internal commercial use.

(3) Consultants, temporary staff, and an outsourced call center fill in forms for the company’s cases in its internal application: internal commercial use.

(4) Customers send documents by email or post, employees enter them into forms, and customers receive letters and status emails: internal commercial use.

(5) An accounting firm’s staff fill in forms for its clients’ work in its internal application, and the clients receive reports but do not sign in: internal commercial use.

(6) The IT company of a group runs the application on the servers of its Affiliates for their employees: internal commercial use.

(7) Customers fill in and submit application, claim, or order forms on a website or in a mobile application that is part of the Licensed Product: Public Web Application / SaaS.

(8) Dealers, suppliers, or partners fill in forms in a portal of the company every day: Public Web Application / SaaS.

(9) The company’s public website or mobile application, a separate product that does not contain the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S), sends data to the company’s internal form application through that application’s API: that website or application needs its own license under Section 17.3, and the internal application keeps its internal commercial use.

(10) A vendor’s service lets each of its customers design its own forms, in one instance for all of them or in a separate instance for each customer in the vendor’s cloud account: Public Web Application / SaaS.

(11) A vendor’s service has desktop and mobile applications that contain the SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT PRODUCT(S) and work only with the service: Public Web Application / SaaS.

(12) Partners resell a vendor’s hosted service under their own brands: Public Web Application / SaaS.

(13) A vendor sells software with forms that its customers install on their own servers or in their own cloud accounts, or a desktop application that works without the vendor’s service: OEM use.

(14) A vendor installs and operates its product in each customer’s own cloud account: OEM use.

(15) A vendor runs its product as a service and also installs it at some customers: OEM use, which also covers the service.

(16) A ministry’s shared form system is used by agencies under its direction or supervision that are not its own units under the PUBLIC BODIES paragraph of Section 1.1: OEM use, for the bodies named under that paragraph.

(17) An integrator builds a form application for one client under a contract that assigns it to the client or licenses it only to that client: the client’s license, with the usage type that the client’s use needs.

(18) An integrator installs its own product at several clients: OEM use, or a license for each client’s copy under the INTEGRATORS AND RESELLERS paragraph of Section 1.1.

(19) A customer’s system submits order forms to the Licensed Product through an API: Public Web Application / SaaS; a supplier’s system sends delivery forms the same way in the company’s purchasing process: internal commercial use.

(20) An IT outsourcer runs a company’s internal form application in the outsourcer’s own data center, and only the company’s employees and the outsourcer’s staff who support it use it: internal commercial use.

(21) A ministry’s shared service center fills in forms on other agencies’ cases in its own application; the agencies send documents by email and receive the results, and their staff do not work in it: internal commercial use.

(22) A public body runs a portal where citizens, companies, and other public bodies fill in and submit application forms on the same terms: Public Web Application / SaaS, and those bodies need not be named.

(23) A government IT agency hosts and administers a ministry’s internal form application and changes its forms at the ministry’s request, and no other body fills in forms on its own cases in it: internal commercial use.

